    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ali-salim-al-beidh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ali-salim-al-beidh.jpg" alt="Ali Salim al-Beidh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ali-salim-al-beidh">Ali Salim al-Beidh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ali_Salem_al-Beidh_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> was formed on 22 May 1990 by the union of the Yemen Arab Republic in the north, with its capital at Sanaa, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-yemen">People's Democratic Republic of Yemen</a>, or South Yemen, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>. Both states had proclaimed Yemeni unity as their goal since the early 1970s, but they had fought border wars in 1972 and 1979 and were divided by ideology: the north was a conservative republic dominated by army officers and tribal leaders, the south the only Marxist state in the Arab world. The weakening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, the south's patron, a bloody internal conflict in Aden in January 1986 and the discovery of oil near the common border made union attractive to both leaderships. Talks moved quickly from late 1989, and unity was proclaimed ahead of the agreed timetable. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-abdullah-saleh">Ali Abdullah Saleh</a>, president of the north since 1978, became chairman of a five-member Presidential Council; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-salim-al-beidh">Ali Salim al-Beidh</a>, secretary-general of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemeni-socialist-party">Yemeni Socialist Party</a>, became vice-president; and Sanaa became the capital, with Aden designated the economic capital. A constitution approved by referendum in May 1991 promised multiparty democracy. The two armies and much of the two administrations, however, were never fully merged, leaving the union fragile from the start.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/marib"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marib.jpg" alt="Marib" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/marib">Marib</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MaribCity001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">بدرالدين الشهاري, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> had about twelve million people in 1990, and its population has since grown to well over thirty million. Most Yemenis lived in villages, farming terraced highlands and wadis, and society in the north was organised around tribes, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hashid">Hashid</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakil">Bakil</a> confederations. Religiously the country divides between Zaydi Shia Muslims in the northern highlands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslims in the south, along the coastal plain and in the east. The economy depended on oil exported from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marib">Marib</a> field from 1987 and the Masila field in Hadramawt from 1993, which provided most government revenue, and on remittances from workers in the Gulf. That second pillar was badly damaged in 1990, when Yemen's refusal to back military action against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> led Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> to expel some 800,000 or more Yemeni workers. Oil output peaked in the early 2000s and then declined, exports of liquefied natural gas from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balhaf">Balhaf</a> began only in 2009, and fuel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a> absorbed a large share of the budget. The cultivation of qat, the mildly stimulant leaf chewed at daily social gatherings, consumed much of the scarce <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a>, and Sanaa faced acute water shortages. Even before the war Yemen was the poorest country in the Arab world, with high rates of illiteracy, unemployment and child malnutrition.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hussein-badreddin-al-houthi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hussein-badreddin-al-houthi.jpg" alt="Hussein Badreddin al-Houthi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hussein-badreddin-al-houthi">Hussein Badreddin al-Houthi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hussein_housi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Elnur777536276, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The armies of north and south kept separate commands after 1990, and their rivalry became open war in 1994. After months of political crisis, including the assassination of southern politicians and a reconciliation accord signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amman">Amman</a> in February 1994 that was never implemented, major clashes began in late April and became full-scale war in May. On 21 May <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-salim-al-beidh">al-Beidh</a> proclaimed a breakaway <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-yemen">Democratic Republic of Yemen</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>, but it won no international recognition, and northern forces, joined by tribal levies and Islamist volunteers, captured Aden on 7 July 1994. Victory gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-abdullah-saleh">Saleh</a> control of the whole country, but southern officers were purged and southern grievances festered, giving rise to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-movement-yemen">Southern Movement</a>, or <em>al-Hirak</em>, in 2007. In the north the government fought six wars between 2004 and 2010 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/houthi-movement">Houthi movement</a>, a Zaydi revivalist group whose founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-badreddin-al-houthi">Hussein Badreddin al-Houthi</a> was killed in 2004; Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> joined the fighting in 2009. Militant jihadism opened a further front: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaeda">al-Qaeda</a> bombed the USS <em>Cole</em> in Aden harbour in October 2000, killing seventeen American sailors, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaeda-in-the-arabian-peninsula">Al-Qaeda in the Arabian Peninsula</a>, formed in 2009, became a target of American drone strikes. Units commanded by Saleh's relatives, above all the Republican Guard under his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmed-ali-abdullah-saleh">Ahmed Ali</a>, grew into the regime's strongest forces.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abdullah-ibn-husayn-al-ahmar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abdullah-ibn-husayn-al-ahmar.jpg" alt="Abdullah ibn Husayn al-Ahmar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abdullah-ibn-husayn-al-ahmar">Abdullah ibn Husayn al-Ahmar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Ahmar_1965_Crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The unified republic began with real pluralism by regional standards. Dozens of parties were licensed, the press became freer, and in April 1993 the first multiparty parliamentary elections gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-abdullah-saleh">Saleh</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/general-peoples-congress">General People's Congress</a> the most seats, followed by the Islamist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-islah-yemen">Islah</a> party, led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hashid">Hashid</a> paramount sheikh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdullah-ibn-husayn-al-ahmar">Abdullah ibn Husayn al-Ahmar</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemeni-socialist-party">Yemeni Socialist Party</a>. After 1994 power steadily concentrated in Saleh's hands. Constitutional amendments abolished the Presidential Council in favour of a directly elected president, and Saleh won elections in 1999 and 2006, the second against a credible opposition candidate, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faisal-bin-shamlan">Faisal bin Shamlan</a>. Amendments in 2001 lengthened presidential and parliamentary terms. Saleh likened governing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> to dancing on the heads of snakes, and he ruled through a web of patronage that bound tribal sheikhs, army commanders, businessmen and party leaders to the presidency, while relatives held key military and security posts. Formal institutions, including parliament, the courts and local councils elected from 2001, existed alongside customary tribal law and mediation, which remained the main means of settling disputes in much of the country. The state's reach was limited outside the cities, and kidnappings of foreigners by tribesmen seeking concessions from the government were frequent in the 1990s.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/shibam"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shibam.jpg" alt="Shibam" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/shibam">Shibam</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%85%D8%AF%D9%8A%D9%86%D8%A9_%D8%B4%D8%A8%D8%A7%D9%85_%D8%AD%D8%B6%D8%B1%D9%85%D9%88%D8%AA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bkar6190, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Unification in 1990 realised a goal that both Yemeni states had professed for decades, and for its first years the republic was the most open political system on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a>, with competitive elections, opposition parties, women voting and standing for parliament, and a relatively free press. The new state brought together a land of deep historical roots, home of the ancient kingdoms of Saba and Himyar, whose old cities of Sanaa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shibam">Shibam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabid">Zabid</a> are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Sites</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>'s position on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bab-el-mandeb">Bab al-Mandab</a> strait, through which much of the trade between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> passes, has given it strategic weight out of proportion to its wealth. The settlement of its borders, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> in 1992, with Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-jeddah-2000">Treaty of Jeddah</a> in 2000 and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> after arbitration over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanish-islands">Hanish Islands</a> in 1998–1999, ended long-running disputes. The country's later collapse has had wide regional consequences: the war turned Yemen into an arena of rivalry between Saudi Arabia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and from late 2023 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/houthi-movement">Houthi</a> attacks on shipping in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> disrupted global trade and drew American and British air strikes. The Yemeni diaspora, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, carries the country's culture far beyond its borders.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ali-mohsen-al-ahmar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ali-mohsen-al-ahmar.jpg" alt="Ali Mohsen al-Ahmar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ali-mohsen-al-ahmar">Ali Mohsen al-Ahmar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henzel_and_al-Ahmar,_2020_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">U.S. Embassy Yemen, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> still exists as an internationally recognised state, but since 2014 it has been divided by war. In 2011 mass protests inspired by the uprisings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> demanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-abdullah-saleh">Saleh</a>'s departure; the army split when General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-mohsen-al-ahmar">Ali Mohsen al-Ahmar</a> sided with the protesters, and Saleh was badly injured in a bomb attack on his palace mosque in June. Under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-cooperation-council">Gulf Cooperation Council</a> initiative signed in November 2011 he handed power to his vice-president, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-rabbuh-mansur-hadi">Abd Rabbuh Mansur Hadi</a>, who was confirmed as sole candidate in February 2012. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-dialogue-conference-yemen">National Dialogue Conference</a> in 2013–2014 proposed a federal state, but the transition faltered. In September 2014 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/houthi-movement">Houthis</a>, tacitly aided by forces loyal to Saleh, seized Sanaa; Hadi fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a>, and in March 2015 a Saudi-led coalition intervened. The Houthis killed Saleh in December 2017 after he broke with them. War, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>, the collapse of the currency and cholera epidemics caused one of the world's worst humanitarian crises, and a UN-commissioned study estimated some 377,000 direct and indirect deaths by the end of 2021. Today the Houthis rule Sanaa and most of the populous north, while the recognised government, led since April 2022 by a Presidential Leadership Council under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashad-al-alimi">Rashad al-Alimi</a>, holds the south and east, where in January 2026 Saudi-backed forces took Aden from the separatist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-transitional-council">Southern Transitional Council</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, created in 1990 by the union of the conservative north and the Marxist south, briefly offered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a>'s most open politics but, weakened by the 1994 civil war, patronage rule, poverty and insurgency, collapsed into a devastating multi-sided war after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/houthi-movement">Houthis</a> seized Sanaa in 2014.</em></p>

